course: language
lesson: 18
title: Accents and dialects of Britain and Ireland
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An accent is how people pronounce words; a dialect is a whole local way of speaking, with its own words and sometimes its own grammar; Britain and Ireland are packed with them, sometimes changing every few miles.
explain: Take a trip: in Liverpool something great is "boss, la", in Newcastle "howay, it's canny good" means come on, this is really good, in Aberdeen "Fit like?" means how are you, and in Dublin a night of "great craic" was great fun.
explain: Even small things have many names: a narrow passage between houses is a ginnel or a snicket in the North, a twitten in Sussex and an alley in many other places.
explain: Most British people do not speak the "posh" BBC accent (Received Pronunciation); it is often estimated that only about 2 or 3 in every 100 people speak it naturally, and the rest have regional accents.
explain: No accent is "lazy" or "the worst": each has its own history and rules, and polls that rank accents mostly show people's prejudices; Scots, spoken in Scotland, is even counted as a language closely related to English.
fact: An accent is the way people pronounce words; a dialect is a local way of speaking with its own words and grammar.
fact: Scouse is the accent and dialect of Liverpool, shaped over centuries partly by Irish and Welsh speech; "boss" means great and "la" means lad or mate.
fact: Geordie is the dialect of Newcastle and keeps some very old English words; "howay" means come on and "canny" means nice or very.
fact: In the Doric dialect of north-east Scotland "Fit like?" means how are you; "fit" is the local way of saying "what", because wh- often becomes f- there.
fact: In Welsh English "now in a minute" means soon, but not right this second.
fact: Cockney rhyming slang from the East End of London swaps a word for a phrase that rhymes with it: apples and pears means stairs, dog and bone means phone, and butcher's hook means look; often the rhyming word is dropped, as in "have a butcher's".
fact: Rhyming slang is fun history; only some phrases are still common today.
fact: "Tara-a-bit" means goodbye for now in Birmingham and the Black Country; "cheerio" is a goodbye heard across Britain.
fact: In Ireland "craic" means fun or a good time, and "eejit" means idiot but is often said kindly between friends.
fact: Ginnel and snicket are northern English words for a narrow passage between houses; twitten is a Sussex word for a narrow lane.
fact: Received Pronunciation (RP) is the traditional "posh" accent once used on the BBC; it is often estimated that only about 2 or 3 in every 100 British people speak it naturally, though nobody knows the exact number.
fact: In Scots "dinnae" means don't, "fash yersel" means worry yourself, "ken" means know and "whit" means what; Scots is a language closely related to English.
fact: In southern English informal speech "well" can mean very ("well tired"); "very tired" is the standard written form.
exercise: What is the difference between an accent and a dialect? | An accent is pronunciation; a dialect also has its own words and grammar | An accent is used only by posh people, a dialect only by farmers | A dialect is written down, while an accent is only ever spoken aloud | They mean exactly the same thing, just in different countries
exercise: Liam from Liverpool says "That's boss, la!" What does he mean? | That's great, mate! | Take me to see your boss at work right now | That's the lad who is in charge of us | That's lost, so let's go and find it
exercise: Which languages helped shape the Scouse accent over the centuries? | Partly Irish and Welsh speech | Mostly French and Spanish sailors' speech | Mostly Latin and Greek | Mostly German and Dutch
exercise: Uncle Ray in Newcastle says "Howay man, it's canny good this!" What does he mean? | Come on, this is really good! | How are you, man, this tin can is good? | Go away, man, this is far too good for you | Hooray, man, this can of drink is mine!
exercise: Isla from Aberdeen says "Fit like?" What is she asking? | How are you? | Are you fit and healthy from running? | Do these new shoes fit you nicely? | Do you like keeping fit at the gym?
exercise: Why does "fit" mean "what" in Doric? | In that dialect wh- often becomes f- | Doric people think "what" is a rude word | It is a spelling mistake in an old book | Fit is a Latin word for what
exercise: Sophie's Welsh friends say "I'll do it now in a minute." When will they do it? | Soon, but not right this very second | Right now, this very second, at once | Never, it's a polite way of saying no | In exactly one minute, timed on a watch
exercise: An East End trader says "Up the apples and pears." Where is he going? | Up the stairs | Up to the fruit stall at the market | Up the apple tree in the garden | Up the road to the greengrocer's
exercise: In Cockney rhyming slang, what is a "dog and bone"? | A phone | A pet | A dinner | A bone
exercise: Is Cockney rhyming slang still used by everyone in London? | No, it's fun history; only some phrases are still common | Yes, every Londoner uses it every single day | Yes, it is taught in every London school by law | No, it was banned by the government years ago
exercise: A Brummie says "Tara-a-bit!" when leaving a shop. What does it mean? | Goodbye for now | Thanks a lot for this | Can I have a bit more? | Wait for me a bit
exercise: A Dublin boy says "That was great craic, ye eejit!" Is his friend being insulted? | No, it's a friendly tease from a mate after a fun night | Yes, it's a serious insult that starts a fight | Yes, craic is a rude word for a broken thing | No, eejit is the Irish word for best friend
exercise: Jess's nan says "ginnel", her friend says "snicket", a girl from Kent says "alley". What are they talking about? | A narrow passage between houses | A small round cake eaten at teatime with jam | A type of garden bird | A short nap after lunch
exercise: About how many British people speak RP naturally? | About 2 or 3 in every 100, by most estimates | Nearly everyone in Britain speaks it from birth | About half of all British people | Only the King and nobody else at all
exercise: A Scottish girl says "Dinnae fash yersel', I ken whit tae dae." What does she mean? | Don't worry yourself, I know what to do | Don't touch that, I need to wash it first | Dinner's ready, come and eat it now | Don't be late, I'm going to Kent today
exercise: Is Scots just English with a funny accent? | No, it's a closely related language with its own words and grammar | Yes, it's exactly English said slowly with a Scottish accent | Yes, it was invented for films about Scotland | No, it is a kind of French
exercise: Jade's Essex mum says "I was well tired." How would school writing put it? | I was very tired | I was good and tired out | I was tired in a healthy way | I was well, then tired
test: Your mate goes "Scousers talk lazy English, innit." What's the fair answer? | Every accent has its own history and rules; none is lazy | He's right, Scouse is lazy because people can't be bothered | He's right, but only when they speak quickly | Scousers speak French, not English
test: A Geordie lad at footie shouts "Howay, that goal was canny!" How did he find the goal? | Really good | Really boring | Very lucky and a bit unfair | Too far away to see
test: A poll says one city's accent is "the worst in Britain". What does that mostly show? | People's prejudices, not anything wrong with the accent | That the people there really cannot speak English properly | That the accent is about to disappear for good | That the accent is the oldest in Britain
test: What would happen if everyone in Britain spoke exactly the same way? | We'd lose lots of local words and history, like ginnel, howay and craic | Nothing, because they already all speak exactly the same way | Everyone would have to learn RP from the King himself | Nobody would ever understand a single word again
test: A Cockney grandad says "Give us a butcher's at your new phone." What does he want? | A look | A sandwich | A meat pie | A sausage
test: Your Welsh auntie says "I'll put the kettle on now in a minute, love." Should you expect tea this second? | Not quite, it'll be soon | Yes, right this very second | No, she means tomorrow morning | No, she's saying she won't make tea at all
test: Your mate thinks most Brits sound like old BBC newsreaders. What's true? | Most British people have regional accents; RP is rare | Yes, nearly everyone in Britain sounds like the BBC | Yes, because RP is taught in every British school | No, everyone in Britain has a Scottish accent
test: In a cartoon, a Scottish character only says "Och aye the noo!" Is that real Scots? | It's a cartoon cliché; real Scots has many words, like ken, dinnae, fash | Yes, that's the only phrase anyone in Scotland ever says | Yes, it means hello and goodbye and nothing else exists | No, real Scots people only speak Gaelic at home
test: A Dublin girl says "Your man there is a total eejit, but he's great craic." ("Your man" means that man.) What does she think of him? | He's silly but loads of fun | He's dangerous and nobody likes him | He's her husband and very serious | He's boring but very clever
test: An Aberdeen boy texts "Fit's fer tea?" ("Fer" is for, "tea" is the evening meal.) What's he asking? | What's for dinner tonight? | Is the tea fit to drink? | Who is coming round for tea? | Is the food fit to eat?
test: Puzzle: in Doric, wh- often becomes f-. What would "Where are you going?" start with? | Far | Wear | Fur | Whar
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a dialect? | A local variety of a language possessing distinct vocabulary and grammar | A defective variety of a language employed by uneducated persons | The manner of pronunciation only, without any lexical distinctions | A language unrelated to any other spoken in the same country
exam: Identify the proportion of the British population estimated to employ Received Pronunciation as a natural accent. | Some two or three in every hundred | Approximately ninety in every hundred | Precisely one half | The entire population without exception
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Cockney rhyming slang. | It substitutes for a word a phrase which rhymes with it, frequently omitting the rhyming element | It consists solely of words borrowed from Latin and Greek | It is mandated in all schools within the city of London | It is a modern invention originating in social media
exam: In respect of the Irish English word "eejit", which statement is most accurate? | It denotes a fool but is frequently employed with affection | It is invariably a grave insult | It denotes a close friend exclusively | It is a term of praise for intelligence
